VI  - 92
IP  - 6
DP  - 2024 Dec 1
TI  - Anesthesia Management of the Patient With Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis 
      Undergoing Lung Lavage.
PG  - 453-458
AB  - Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) is a rare pulmonary disorder characterized 
      by the accumulation of surfactant/lipoprotein material in the alveoli and 
      subsequent hypoxemic respiratory failure. Whole lung lavage (WLL), a procedure 
      used to physically remove the lipoprotein material from the alveoli, is the 
      first-line treatment for this disease process. Anesthesia providers may 
      infrequently encounter the management of the WLL procedure due to the rarity of 
      the underlying disease process. Pertinent anesthesia considerations for WLL are 
      covered in the following case report. A review of the literature examines the 
      pathophysiology of PAP, the various approaches to WLL, and the physiologic 
      implications of WLL.
