VI  - 165
IP  - 1
DP  - 2002 Jan 1
TI  - Successful treatment of endogenous lipoid pneumonia due to Niemann-Pick Type B 
      disease with whole-lung lavage.
PG  - 128-31
AB  - In Type B Niemann-Pick disease, progressive pulmonary infiltration is a major 
      cause of morbidity and mortality, although the disease is usually diagnosed 
      before adulthood in other organ systems. To date, no successful treatment of 
      pulmonary involvement by Niemann-Pick disease has been documented. We describe 
      the case of a patient with Niemann-Pick Type B disease who presented with 
      extensive endogenous lipoid pneumonia and life-threatening hypoxia following 
      bypass grafting for severe coronary artery disease. A surgical lung biopsy at the 
      time of grafting revealed characteristic histology and ultrastructural features 
      of Niemann-Pick disease, with confirmatory findings in biochemical studies. 
      Because of the severity of the patient's symptoms, bilateral whole-lung lavage 
      was undertaken, leading to symptomatic improvement, lessening of parenchymal 
      opacification on high-resolution computed tomographic scanning, and a marked 
      improvement in resting arterial oxygen tension while breathing air to 10.3 kPa 
      from 8.4 kPa. Whole-lung lavage may be a potentially useful modality of treatment 
      for patients with pulmonary involvement by Niemann-Pick Type B disease.
