PMID- 17386098
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20071106
LR  - 20181113
IS  - 1750-1172 (Electronic)
IS  - 1750-1172 (Linking)
VI  - 2
DP  - 2007 Mar 26
TI  - Successful whole lung lavage in pulmonary alveolar proteinosis secondary to 
      lysinuric protein intolerance: a case report.
PG  - 14
AB  - BACKGROUND: Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) is a rare disease characterised 
      by accumulation of lipoproteinaceous material within alveoli, occurring in three 
      clinically distinct forms: congenital, acquired and secondary. Among the latter, 
      lysinuric protein intolerance (LPI) is a rare genetic disorder caused by 
      defective transport of cationic amino acids. Whole Lung Lavage (WLL) is currently 
      the gold standard therapy for severe cases of PAP. CASE PRESENTATION: We describe 
      the case of an Italian boy affected by LPI who, by the age of 10, developed 
      digital clubbing and, by the age of 16, a mild restrictive functional impairment 
      associated with a high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) pattern consistent 
      with pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. After careful assessment, he underwent WLL. 
      CONCLUSION: Two years after WLL, the patient has no clinical, radiological or 
      functional evidence of pulmonary disease recurrence, thus suggesting that WLL may 
      be helpful in the treatment of PAP secondary to LPI.
