PMID- 562090
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 19771125
LR  - 20190703
IS  - 0003-2999 (Print)
IS  - 0003-2999 (Linking)
VI  - 56
IP  - 5
DP  - 1977 Sep-Oct
TI  - Anesthetic management of pulmonary lavage in adults.
PG  - 661-8
AB  - A 6-year experience in the anesthetic management of 34 successful whole-lung 
      lavages on 11 adult patients with pulmonary alveolar proteinosis is described. 
      All patients were radiographically, physiologically, and symptomatically improved 
      after the procedures. The anesthetic protocol for lung lavage includes: (1) 
      unilateral whole-lung lavages 2 to 4 days apart; (2) general anesthesia with the 
      placement of a Carlens tube; (3) isotonic saline as the lavage solution; (4) 
      mechanical chest percussion during lavage; (5) serial arterial blood-gas 
      determination and measurement of lung compliance in the intraoperative and 
      immediate postlavage period. The authors conclude that whole-lung lavage is a 
      safe and effective palliative procedure in pulmonary alveolar proteinosis and in 
      the treatment of patients with pulmonary disease, such as cystic fibrosis or 
      asthma, in which filling of the lung acini by liquid or solid material impairs 
      oxygenation of the pulmonary capillary blood.
