TI  - Role of rituximab as neoadjuvant therapy in a difficult case of relapsing 
      pulmonary alveolar proteinosis.
PG  - 369-371
LID - 10.1016/j.mjafi.2022.10.005 [doi]
AB  - Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) is a rare disease which involves the 
      accumulation of insoluble lipoproteinaceous material in the alveoli leading to 
      impaired gas exchange and even respiratory failure. Autoimmune PAP is the most 
      common type and is characterized by the presence of anti-granulocyte-monocyte 
      colony stimulating factor (anti GM-CSF) antibody. Whole lung lavage has been 
      traditionally used as first-line management of PAP but there is a lack of clarity 
      especially in the treatment of relapsing cases of PAP. Rituximab is an anti 
      Cluster of Differentiate 20 (CD 20) monoclonal antibody that has been tried as 
      salvage therapy for relapsing cases of PAP. We present a case of 35 years old 
      female patient who was diagnosed as a case of relapsing PAP who was managed 
      initially with neoadjuvant rituximab. This is a retrospective observational 
      report showing novel use of neoadjuvant rituximab in a difficult case of 
      relapsing PAP.
