TI  - Hereditary pulmonary alveolar proteinosis in a 5-year-old child: Diagnostic 
      insights and therapeutic approach.
PG  - 3855-3858
LID - 10.1016/j.radcr.2025.04.094 [doi]
AB  - Hereditary pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (hPAP) is a rare disorder caused by 
      mutations in the CSF2RA or CSF2RB genes, leading to impaired surfactant clearance 
      by alveolar macrophages and subsequent respiratory dysfunction. A 5-year-old 
      female with a 2-year history of poor weight gain, fatigue, and intermittent fever 
      was evaluated. Clinical evaluation revealed hypoxemia, while high-resolution 
      computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest showed the characteristic "crazy-paving" 
      pattern suggestive of PAP. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) yielded milky fluid with 
      periodic acid-Schiff (PAS)-positive material, and genetic testing confirmed a 
      homozygous mutation in the CSF2RA gene, consistent with hPAP. The patient 
      underwent therapeutic whole lung lavage (WLL), resulting in significant clinical 
      improvement. This case underscores the challenges of diagnosing pediatric hPAP 
      and the value of integrating imaging, pathology, and genetic testing. While WLL 
      remains the mainstay of treatment, further research is needed to develop targeted 
      therapies.
