TI  - Anesthesia for lung lavage in pediatric patient with pulmonary alveolar 
      proteinosis.
PG  - 269-73
LID - 10.1016/S0034-7094(12)70125-3 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES: Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis (PAP) is a rare 
      disorder first described in 1958. The Whole-Lung Lavage (WLL) proposed in the 
      1960s, remains the treatment of choice. Several techniques have been described to 
      perform lung lavage in pediatric patients; however, all have limitation and 
      risks. CASE REPORT: Female patient, aged 6 years and 8 months, 25 kg, diagnosed 
      with pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, who underwent whole-lung lavage by 
      sequential lobar fiberoptic bronchoscopy under general anesthesia and spontaneous 
      ventilation.
