TI  - Development of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis following exposure to dust after 
      the Great East Japan Earthquake.
PG  - 212-6
LID - S2212-5345(13)00047-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.resinv.2013.04.005 [doi]
AB  - We report a unique case of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis that developed 3 weeks 
      after the Great East Japan Earthquake and the subsequent tsunami. The patient had 
      inhaled dust repeatedly while visiting her devastated neighborhood without 
      wearing a protective mask. Five weeks after the earthquake, lung samples taken 
      from the patient showed foreign particle deposition; however, her serum was 
      negative for GM-CSF autoantibody. The patient's clinical symptoms resolved 
      following whole lung lavage. We conclude that inhalation of fine dust particles 
      after natural disasters may cause the onset of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis.
