PMC - PMC12582097
OAB - Autoimmune Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis (aPAP) is a rare disease where the 
      immune system causes the buildup of an oily substance called surfactant in the 
      lungs. Surfactant usually helps keep tiny sacs of air in the lungs called alveoli 
      open making it easier to breathe. When it builds up it clogs alveoli making it 
      difficult to breathe. This happens because the body produces harmful antibodies 
      against certain proteins involved in controlling surfactant. Previously, 
      treatments for aPAP focused on whole lung lavage which is where the built up 
      surfactant is washed out of the lungs. Recombinant GM-CSF (rGM-CSF) is a new 
      treatment involved in stopping these antibodies causing surfactant to build up. 
      This treatment has shown promise in helping people with aPAP. It is inhaled using 
      a nebulizer and typically has minimal side effects. However, studies are ongoing 
      to confirm how well it works and how safe it is in everyday use. If future 
      research shows benefit, rGM-CSF may become the first-line choice for treating 
      aPAP. This review looks at the studies which have already been done on rGM-CSF, 
      including studies reviewing how the drug behaves in the body, how effective it 
      is, and how safe it is. It also covers research that is ongoing and what the 
      future might look like for this treatment.
